WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.465 --> 00:00:02.613
Hello everyone!

00:00:02.613 --> 00:00:04.904
This is the channel "Math experiments".

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:06.814
Today we have an ambitious task:

00:00:06.814 --> 00:00:12.164
to run a hundred meters from Pascal's triangle to the value of the zeta (ζ) function.

00:00:12.164 --> 00:00:16.380
We will run at a great speed... like Usain Bolt.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:22.328
But in truth, we will not run for 10 seconds, because I'm not Usain Bolt.

00:00:22.328 --> 00:00:28.509
And the distance is not 100 meters. It's more like ten kilometers. Look at how much scrolling is needed...

00:00:28.509 --> 00:00:30.739
Well, actually, it's not that much...

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:33.592
I tried to find the shortest path

00:00:33.592 --> 00:00:40.860
from the simple and understandable Pascal's triangle to the great and incomprehensible zeta (ζ) function.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.743
Why is it great and incomprehensible?

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:48.000
There are many interesting videos about the zeta function

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.995
and, of course, you should start with the value of the zeta function at 2.

00:00:54.995 --> 00:00:58.396
Calculating the value of the zeta function at 2 is a separate task.

00:00:58.396 --> 00:01:02.132
Calculating the value of the zeta function at 3 is another task.

00:01:02.132 --> 00:01:07.509
Calculating the zeros of the zeta function is also a serious task.

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:11.834
Proving that they are located here and there is the greatest task of modern times.

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:14.020
But I digress...

00:01:15.086 --> 00:01:19.104
The value of the zeta function is defined as follows:

00:01:19.104 --> 00:01:21.249
it is the sum of the reciprocals of the k-th powers.

00:01:21.249 --> 00:01:23.797
This is the value of the zeta function at point k.

00:01:23.797 --> 00:01:27.228
The sum of the reciprocals of the k-th powers of natural numbers.

00:01:27.228 --> 00:01:33.271
Hence, the sum of the reciprocals of the squares is the value of the zeta function at point 2.

00:01:33.271 --> 00:01:36.000
And this value is Pi^2/6.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.838
This problem, to calculate the sum of inverse squares, is known as the Basel problem.

00:01:41.838 --> 00:01:48.000
Interestingly, it was posed to mathematicians for 45 years.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.309
For 45 years, people couldn't find what this sum equaled.

00:01:51.309 --> 00:01:54.497
It's amusing to think about this in our days.

00:01:54.497 --> 00:01:59.451
Apparently, in mathematics, as in computer science, there's an acceleration of progress.

00:01:59.451 --> 00:02:02.517
Every year, acceleration increases and speed increases.

00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:12.436
Everything increases, and what happened 100 or 200 years ago seems amusing.

00:02:13.807 --> 00:02:16.614
In general, in those times people didn't know what Fourier transforms were.

00:02:16.614 --> 00:02:21.416
They didn't know enough about the series... How to properly deal with them.

00:02:22.269 --> 00:02:26.969
And they couldn't calculate this sum.

00:02:26.969 --> 00:02:29.274
They had to be tricky about it.

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:36.573
And there are many interesting videos about how to calculate this sum of inverse squares and get that coveted Pi^2/(6).

00:02:36.573 --> 00:02:45.261
You have to find some connection to the circle, to trigonometric functions, to make Pi^2/(6) appear.

00:02:45.261 --> 00:02:51.007
And I recommend looking at the channel, for example, zetamath.

00:02:51.007 --> 00:02:53.677
It's in English, though...

00:02:53.677 --> 00:02:57.992
If your English isn't good enough to watch lectures in English,

00:02:57.992 --> 00:03:03.707
I recommend installing Yandex Browser, opening YouTube, hovering over the video, and finding the icon with hieroglyphs.

00:03:03.707 --> 00:03:07.473
Click on it and you will get synchronized translation in Russian.

00:03:07.473 --> 00:03:14.072
This is cool. It greatly expands the amount of mathematical educational content

00:03:14.072 --> 00:03:17.321
that you can consume for self-improvement.

00:03:17.321 --> 00:03:19.920
This is really... this is ridiculously cool.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.945
Well, you can also learn English. That's also an option.

00:03:24.945 --> 00:03:32.579
Today, I'm not talking about the function Zeta[2] = Pi^2/(6),

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:43.229
but about how we suddenly, completely accidentally, stumble upon the values of the zeta function -1, -2, -3,

00:03:43.229 --> 00:03:45.625
solving some simple problem.

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:51.217
By doing some simple exercises, summing this series.

00:03:51.217 --> 00:03:56.568
Our task is to find the sum of this finite series of k-th powers of natural numbers.

00:03:56.568 --> 00:03:58.568
Not all of them, but up to the number n.

00:03:58.568 --> 00:04:05.327
In mathematics, by the way, this sum is known as HarmonicNumber[m, -k].

00:04:07.154 --> 00:04:09.813
And...

00:04:09.813 --> 00:04:12.635
Maybe some of you have already thought about it.

00:04:12.635 --> 00:04:19.112
What is Zeta[-2]? This is when k = -2, which means the sum of the squares of all natural numbers.

00:04:19.112 --> 00:04:24.000
This is a very large number, namely - infinity.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.903
Indeed, it's somewhat incomprehensible until you learn what analytic continuation of a function is.

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:32.487
But I won't talk about it now either.

00:04:32.487 --> 00:04:39.474
We'll just accidentally stumble upon some numbers that will be called Zeta[-1], Zeta[-2], Zeta[-3]...

00:04:39.474 --> 00:04:43.033
and someday I might be able to tell you more about it.

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:45.371
And now, let's start again...

00:04:45.371 --> 00:04:47.829
Pascal's triangle. Finally!

00:04:47.829 --> 00:04:52.355
We return to simple and understandable things for us - Pascal's triangle.

00:04:52.355 --> 00:04:55.360
It arises when you expand (a+b)^n.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.279
Or like here (1+x)^n.

00:05:00.436 --> 00:05:02.039
I want to make a small transformation.

00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:05.105
Let's move this highest power of x^5 to the left.

00:05:05.105 --> 00:05:07.470
Move x^4 to the left.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.470
This is what we get.

00:05:10.719 --> 00:05:16.324
On the right, we have this truncated Pascal's triangle with the right slope cut off.

00:05:17.238 --> 00:05:19.276
And what I want to do here is...

00:05:19.276 --> 00:05:26.058
I want to take these three equations...

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:27.880
The arrow means "equals."

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:33.387
Three equations and name them, give them names a3, a2, a1.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.034
And I want to sum them with certain coefficients.

00:05:38.034 --> 00:05:46.155
I want only x^2 to remain on the right, and all other terms (constant terms and terms with x) to disappear.

00:05:46.155 --> 00:05:47.546
This is done very simply:

00:05:47.546 --> 00:05:50.665
Take the first equation, multiply by 2. Multiply the second equation by 3.

00:05:50.665 --> 00:05:52.482
And subtract the second equation from the first.

00:05:52.482 --> 00:05:57.153
Here it is written like this: 2a3 - 3a2...

00:05:57.153 --> 00:06:02.223
It turns out that 3x^2 becomes 6x^2,

00:06:02.223 --> 00:06:06.782
and 3x becomes 6x, 2x becomes 6x, they cancel out.

00:06:06.782 --> 00:06:16.274
And we're left with 2 - 3. And we need to eliminate the remaining -1 term. And for this we add a1.

00:06:16.274 --> 00:06:21.685
This is the linear combination of three equations that we're interested in.

00:06:21.685 --> 00:06:24.000
And this is what we get.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.387
On the right, we get what is written here.

00:06:29.387 --> 00:06:31.658
6x^2. That's correct!

00:06:31.658 --> 00:06:33.841
Only x^2 remains on the right.

00:06:33.841 --> 00:06:36.937
Let's divide by 6 and this is what we have.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.908
Of course, you might ask: why to do all this?

00:06:44.126 --> 00:06:45.481
I don't like when viewers don't understand what happens on the screen.

00:06:45.481 --> 00:06:52.972
It is important that all actions that are taken are understood for why they are being done.

00:06:52.972 --> 00:07:01.847
And I had started telling you about some manipulations with three equations for some reason.

00:07:01.847 --> 00:07:05.165
But wait... you'll understand it all soon.

00:07:05.165 --> 00:07:06.476
In short...

00:07:06.476 --> 00:07:14.232
Actually, what I want to do is find the sum of 1^2 + 2^2 + 3^2 ... + n^2

00:07:14.902 --> 00:07:20.233
And there are many ways to obtain this result.

00:07:20.233 --> 00:07:22.142
I'm just telling you one of them.

00:07:22.142 --> 00:07:30.325
Here it is: take Pascal's triangle and do some manipulations to make only x^2 remain on the right.

00:07:30.325 --> 00:07:33.054
And look at what we get afterwards:

00:07:33.054 --> 00:07:41.541
I take the obtained equation, one, and add it to itself by substituting x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 ... n

00:07:42.363 --> 00:07:47.269
Then on the right, I'll have the sum of the squares of the first n natural numbers.

00:07:47.269 --> 00:07:49.009
And on the left... What will be on the left?

00:07:49.009 --> 00:07:50.028
You probably guessed already.

00:07:50.028 --> 00:07:55.865
This bracket will be repeated several times for different values of x.

00:07:58.657 --> 00:08:02.302
There. I added this equation to itself four times

00:08:02.302 --> 00:08:06.542
and you can see how the terms cancel out when we sum up this column.

00:08:06.542 --> 00:08:08.189
1^3 - 0^3

00:08:08.189 --> 00:08:09.793
2^3 - 1^3

00:08:09.793 --> 00:08:11.312
3^3 - 2^3

00:08:11.312 --> 00:08:12.831
4^3 - 3^3

00:08:12.831 --> 00:08:16.293
and when they cancel out, we're left with just (1 + 3)^3

00:08:17.115 --> 00:08:18.470
Similarly here.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:22.602
When we sum up these terms with -3,

00:08:22.602 --> 00:08:27.313
we'll just get -3(1+3)^2

00:08:28.135 --> 00:08:35.020
In short, when we add this equation to itself n times...
In short, when we add this equation to itself n times...

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.477
n plus 1 times, starting from zero,

00:08:37.477 --> 00:08:39.660
we get the following equation,

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:45.619
that the sum of the squares of the natural numbers from 0 to n

00:08:45.619 --> 00:08:48.837
equals this quantity.

00:08:49.933 --> 00:08:54.522
Let's expand this expression. This is what it's equal to.

00:08:54.522 --> 00:08:59.520
This is a formula for the sum of the first n squares of natural numbers.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:05.451
Remarkably, this sum coincides with the third degree Bernoulli polynomial.

00:09:05.451 --> 00:09:10.863
Well, because Bernoulli polynomials are defined that way.

00:09:10.863 --> 00:09:19.472
More precisely, not quite, but for the number three they coincide completely...

00:09:19.472 --> 00:09:22.493
However, you need to divide by 3.

00:09:22.493 --> 00:09:24.188
For four...

00:09:24.188 --> 00:09:26.858
For four there is some difference.

00:09:26.858 --> 00:09:28.300
Let's see...

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:39.033
HarmonicNumber for the value (n, -2) is precisely the 3-degree Bernoulli polynomial.

00:09:39.033 --> 00:09:43.744
But let's take a look, for example, if this is true for the sum of fourth powers.

00:09:43.744 --> 00:09:48.759
Mathematics knows what the sum of fourth powers is.

00:09:48.759 --> 00:09:56.509
Is it true that it's the fifth degree Bernoulli polynomial?

00:09:56.509 --> 00:09:58.509
Yes, that's correct.

00:09:58.509 --> 00:10:00.988
Only you have to divide it by k.

00:10:01.548 --> 00:10:03.631
And for the third degree...

00:10:03.631 --> 00:10:08.370
If we sum up the third degree, we get the fourth degree polynomial,

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:12.837
and the fourth degree Bernoulli polynomial is a different expression.

00:10:12.837 --> 00:10:17.032
It coincides in the leading terms, but differs in the lower ones.

00:10:17.032 --> 00:10:21.438
What is this connected to? It's connected to the fact that Bernoulli polynomials are normalized in a certain way,

00:10:21.438 --> 00:10:26.697
namely, they are normalized so that the integral from 0 to 1 is equal to zero for all Bernoulli polynomials.

00:10:26.697 --> 00:10:29.005
But that's not important. Let's talk later about it.

00:10:30.406 --> 00:10:39.685
Bernoulli polynomials allow us to calculate sums of k-th powers of consecutive natural numbers from one arbitrary number to another.

00:10:39.685 --> 00:10:42.706
This is how it's done.

00:10:42.706 --> 00:10:47.009
Mathematica is great at computing these polynomials.

00:10:47.009 --> 00:10:50.745
You can ask it to factor them.

00:10:50.745 --> 00:10:54.268
Like this, you can compute the roots directly.

00:10:54.268 --> 00:10:56.268
Or you can compute them numerically.

00:10:56.268 --> 00:10:59.395
And our roots turn out to be complex.

00:11:00.309 --> 00:11:08.952
At first we got lucky and all roots were real, although irrational,

00:11:08.952 --> 00:11:21.521
but as n increases, starting from k = 6, we already get complex roots.

00:11:21.521 --> 00:11:25.832
These roots can be visualized...

00:11:25.832 --> 00:11:27.832
like this.

00:11:28.411 --> 00:11:30.000
Let me delete...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.848
These roots.

00:11:31.848 --> 00:11:40.914
I add two slashes "//" and get a list consisting of two lists.

00:11:40.914 --> 00:11:44.010
The first one is the real parts of the roots.

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:47.228
The second one is the imaginary parts of the roots.

00:11:47.228 --> 00:11:54.131
Well, we need to do Transpose to get a list of pairs instead of a list of two lists.

00:11:54.131 --> 00:11:57.977
Real coordinate and imaginary coordinate.

00:11:57.977 --> 00:12:04.175
And let's add "//" and ListPlot here.

00:12:04.967 --> 00:12:15.584
"//" in Mathematica language means to compute what's on the left and apply the function described on the right to it.

00:12:15.584 --> 00:12:16.762
We did that here.

00:12:16.762 --> 00:12:23.818
N - means to obtain the numerical value of what's on the left.

00:12:23.818 --> 00:12:26.092
This function that I've constructed by myself.

00:12:26.092 --> 00:12:32.491
In programming, such constructs are known as lambda(λ) expressions.

00:12:35.817 --> 00:12:39.472
Here instead of the hash sign (#) comes everything on the left, and on the left is a list.

00:12:39.472 --> 00:12:45.614
The function "Re" applied to a list becomes a list of "Re" applied to individual components of the list.

00:12:45.614 --> 00:12:48.832
Therefore, what we get is two lists,

00:12:48.832 --> 00:12:52.895
but when we transpose them, we get a list of pairs.

00:12:52.895 --> 00:12:57.024
There we go. We have drawn it! Beauty rules, doesn't it?

00:12:57.024 --> 00:13:00.501
We just need some grid lines.

00:13:00.501 --> 00:13:06.128
I don't like plots without grid lines.

00:13:06.128 --> 00:13:12.749
And of course, we need "Manipulate." Since we're in Mathematica, we might as well run "Manipulate."

00:13:13.785 --> 00:13:16.547
Beauty rules!

00:13:16.547 --> 00:13:19.521
We have got such two forms.

00:13:21.035 --> 00:13:22.561
So there you have it!

00:13:22.561 --> 00:13:26.589
In reality, we've been digressing. We haven't been running straight towards our goal.

00:13:26.589 --> 00:13:32.244
We've been zig-zagging towards the roots of the Bernoulli polynomials.

00:13:32.244 --> 00:13:36.548
So here's our Pascal's Triangle.

00:13:36.548 --> 00:13:42.777
What did we do when we looked for the Bernoulli polynomial of degree three?

00:13:42.777 --> 00:13:52.237
Actually, we took a certain row from some inverse matrix, and now I'll explain what all of this means.

00:13:53.516 --> 00:14:00.633
This Pascal's Triangle, written in this form, can be turned into a matrix.

00:14:00.633 --> 00:14:04.325
An n-by-n matrix.

00:14:04.325 --> 00:14:08.427
Where n here is equal to 10.

00:14:08.427 --> 00:14:14.409
There you have it. This is Pascal's Triangle with its right slope cut off.

00:14:14.409 --> 00:14:16.157
And...

00:14:16.705 --> 00:14:20.469
Another representation is the more traditional one, with round brackets.

00:14:20.469 --> 00:14:26.093
That's how matrices are usually depicted. With big round brackets on either side.

00:14:27.281 --> 00:14:29.699
And this equality...

00:14:29.699 --> 00:14:31.794
Well, not just one equality, but a set of equalities.

00:14:31.794 --> 00:14:34.647
...can be represented as a single equality.

00:14:36.322 --> 00:14:42.129
Formally as a single equality, the meaning of which is as follows:

00:14:42.129 --> 00:14:48.000
A column matrix equals a square matrix multiplied by a column.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.037
This is the formal notation for the following:

00:14:50.037 --> 00:14:58.516
We take this column and multiply it element-wise by the first row.

00:14:58.516 --> 00:14:59.199
Just like that.

00:14:59.199 --> 00:15:00.306
1 goes here

00:15:00.306 --> 00:15:01.674
x goes here

00:15:01.674 --> 00:15:03.558
x^2 goes here.

00:15:03.558 --> 00:15:06.441
And we multiply,

00:15:06.441 --> 00:15:11.669
and then we replace the spaces here with a plus sign and add them up.

00:15:11.669 --> 00:15:15.770
And this should be equal to this value.

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:17.506
This value is equal to this thing:

00:15:17.506 --> 00:15:22.590
We take this column,

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:29.402
put it this time on the second row,

00:15:29.402 --> 00:15:31.334
multiply element-wise,

00:15:31.334 --> 00:15:36.806
and then add up, replacing these spaces with a plus sign, and so on.

00:15:37.659 --> 00:15:39.193
That is...

00:15:39.193 --> 00:15:43.325
It's not magic, it's just a formal notation for what is shown above.

00:15:43.325 --> 00:15:44.503
No more than that.

00:15:44.503 --> 00:15:49.200
Just a short way to write what is on top.

00:15:50.936 --> 00:15:56.520
But in addition to being a formal notation, it's also an interesting problem.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:01.709
We have this formal notation, and I want to get a formal notation in the other direction.

00:16:01.709 --> 00:16:09.345
I want to represent this column as a certain square matrix multiplied by this column.

00:16:09.345 --> 00:16:11.281
In the other direction. Reverse.

00:16:11.281 --> 00:16:13.981
Here, this column is expressed through this one,

00:16:13.981 --> 00:16:15.068
and I want to go in the other direction.

00:16:15.068 --> 00:16:19.199
This operation is called matrix inversion.

00:16:19.199 --> 00:16:20.621
And what have we done?

00:16:20.621 --> 00:16:26.151
Essentially, we have found the value of this inverse matrix in this row.

00:16:26.151 --> 00:16:31.562
We know that its values are 1/6, -1/2, 1/3.

00:16:31.562 --> 00:16:32.781
Why?

00:16:32.781 --> 00:16:37.027
Because this expression...

00:16:37.027 --> 00:16:39.027
No, not that...

00:16:40.428 --> 00:16:50.017
Because our Bernoulli polynomial is 1/6 before n, -1/2 before n^2, and 1/3 before n^3.

00:16:53.063 --> 00:16:55.983
Let's do...

00:16:55.983 --> 00:16:57.953
matrix inversion.

00:16:57.953 --> 00:17:04.765
For this, Mathematica has the Inverse function, and we got our values of 1/6, -1/2, 1/3.

00:17:06.714 --> 00:17:07.653
So...

00:17:07.653 --> 00:17:12.315
First of all, trust me, you can do the fourth line yourself,

00:17:12.315 --> 00:17:21.617
since we did it for the third line, you can try to find the fourth line yourself and you'll succeed.

00:17:21.617 --> 00:17:27.790
You will need to take not...

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:32.897
not these three equations, but four.

00:17:32.897 --> 00:17:38.399
And your task will be to find a linear combination of four equations

00:17:38.399 --> 00:17:41.739
and get x^3 on the right.

00:17:41.739 --> 00:17:44.451
At first, you'll understand how to eliminate x^2.

00:17:44.451 --> 00:17:47.395
For this, you need to multiply this by 2...

00:17:47.395 --> 00:17:50.918
and subtract it from this.

00:17:50.918 --> 00:17:54.989
Then you can forget about this equation

00:17:54.989 --> 00:17:56.999
and work on subtracting this equation from that one,

00:17:56.999 --> 00:18:00.887
which resulted here and eliminate the x component.

00:18:00.887 --> 00:18:05.415
Well, and then eliminate the constant term by subtracting the required amount of this equation.

00:18:05.415 --> 00:18:07.381
In short, clear.

00:18:07.381 --> 00:18:09.564
Okay.

00:18:09.564 --> 00:18:14.000
You are capable of taking and calculating this matrix yourself.

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:15.127
Completely.

00:18:15.127 --> 00:18:16.907
With a bit effort.

00:18:17.577 --> 00:18:20.029
Let's take a closer look at this matrix.

00:18:21.333 --> 00:18:26.976
So, I wanted to make it look beautiful here. I drew faint borders.

00:18:26.976 --> 00:18:28.865
Well, okay...

00:18:28.865 --> 00:18:36.877
Let's see what's drawn here, for example, in the 9th row.

00:18:36.877 --> 00:18:40.613
-1/30, 2/9, -7/15

00:18:40.613 --> 00:18:46.298
These are actually the coefficients of the Bernoulli polynomial divided by 9.

00:18:46.298 --> 00:18:48.298
Alright!

00:18:48.907 --> 00:18:49.529
Here...

00:18:49.529 --> 00:18:52.709
Here I decided to draw more.

00:18:52.709 --> 00:18:56.987
On this picture, you can already look for patterns.

